  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An upper ridge centered over the Southeast CONUS and \nsfc high pressure ridging in from the north will keep generally \ndry conditions across ENC through friday afternoon. Sea breeze \nis progressing through the region as weak synoptic flow provides\nless resistance compared to the past couple days. We have \nwarmed up to the upper 80s inland, low 80s for beaches with \ntemps expected to quickly drop around sunset. One more night in \nthe 60s for tonight before TD's surge more on Friday as a\nprefrontal trough moves through and high pressure weakens and \nslides further offshore in the morning hours. Trough brings\nlight NW winds behind it briefly, before the front moves through\nFriday night ushering in more NE winds and precip chances.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front is progged to move in late Friday and \nFriday night. Sfc convergence and lowering heights aloft as \nshortwave energy rides over the upper ridge along with increasing \nmoisture will allow for a better chance of showers and perhaps a few \nstorms acrs nrn half of the FA, with best chances N of Hwy 264 \nFriday afternoon into evening. Front slowly sags into srn portions \nof ENC Sat, with best chances for showers and iso thunderstorms for \nsrn areas on Sat. Fcst a bit more muddled beyond Sat as it will\ndepend on the front location. Temps rise to above climo once \nagain Fri into early next week.\n\nNext week, a strong high pressure system centered north of the Great \nLakes region shifts east, tightening the pressure gradient along the \nmid-atlantic and northeast as it pushes against a non-tropical low \nforming offshore. This will likely usher in stronger NE winds to the \nregion. Given it is 5-6 days out, there is a margin for error, \nbut for now there is good model consensus on higher waves and \nelevated winds along the Outer Banks mid- next week. This will \nbe worth keeping an eye on, as a long fetch of strong winds \ncould bring higher wave energy towards vulnerable portions of \nthe Outer Banks. It is too soon to determine specifics at this \npoint and trends will have to be monitored in the coming days.",
